//Understanding PTR{Pointers in Assembly and addressing}

The Code to analyse:

<+0>:     endbr64 
<+4>:     push   rbp
<+5>:     mov    rbp,rsp
<+8>:     mov    DWORD PTR [rbp-0x14],edi
<+11>:    mov    QWORD PTR [rbp-0x20],rsi
<+15>:    mov    DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4],0x9fe1a
<+22>:    mov    eax,DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4]
<+25>:    pop    rbp
<+26>:    ret


What does this code do:
1)endbr64 - some security thing in intel syntax of assembly, ignore for this challenge
2)push rbp - push rbp value in rbp into the stack 
3) mov rbp, rsp - copy value of stack pointer RSP into rbp BASE POINTER
4)mov DWORD PTR [rbp-0x14],edi - move the value of edi into DWORD(4bytes) PTR pointing t [rbp-0x14] -> 14 bits before rbp
5)mov QWORD PTR [rbp-0x20],rsi - similar to DWORD
6)mov DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4],0x9fe1a -similar to above 
7)eax,DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4] - the value stored in eax is stored in DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4] and this pointer contains value -->{0x9fe1a}

